**Read:** [Electromagnetism — David Tong](../sources/Electromagnetism-Tong.lingua#r_278NEA0Z85RJZSPXQ8G55E3SR1), potentials, gauges and wave equations; [Classical Electromagnetism — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Classical-Electromagnetism-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_7NH7AZZH3NZ2FHJ4K5VBT49F6H), gauge transformations and retarded potentials.

Write B = ∇×A and E = −∇φ − ∂A/∂t. The transformation A′ = A + ∇χ and φ′ = φ − ∂χ/∂t leaves both fields unchanged. Verify this directly with commuting derivatives before selecting a gauge for a calculation.

Coulomb and Lorenz gauges organize source and propagation equations differently. The Lorenz condition ∇·A + (1/c²)∂φ/∂t = 0 produces wave equations for the potentials in vacuum. Boundary/initial conditions and residual gauge freedom still matter. An apparently instantaneous scalar-potential contribution in one representation does not establish superluminal physical signalling; the complete E field includes the vector-potential term.

Construct two representations of the same simple field and show identical force and energy observables. Derive the retarded Green solution and contrast it with advanced or time-symmetric mathematical solutions whose selection requires physical boundary conditions. A numerical gauge choice can affect conditioning and constraint handling without changing exact observables. Keep potentials, gauge-dependent intermediate quantities and measured field responses distinct throughout later radiation and inverse studies.

